# Game Architecture

## Layering — keep logic pure

- `src/game/` — pure TypeScript game logic. **No React, no Skia, no Reanimated
  imports here.** Plain data + pure functions so the simulation is testable and
  deterministic.
- `src/rendering/` — Skia components that draw the game state. The whole game
  renders on **one Skia `<Canvas>`**.
- `src/screens/` — React screens (Game, Home, Death overlay).
- `src/state/` — zustand stores for app-level state only (screen, settings,
  best score). **Never put per-frame game state in zustand or React state.**

## Worklet rules (crash-critical)

Game functions carry a `'worklet'` directive so they run on the UI thread.
The worklets runtime in Expo Go SDK 54 (react-native-worklets 0.5.x) captures a
worklet's same-module references at definition time — **a worklet calling another
function in the same file must be defined AFTER its callee**. Order every module
callees-first, entry points last. Getting this wrong is not a red-box: it
hard-crashes Expo Go on launch ("X is not a function (it is undefined)" thrown
inside the UI-thread frame callback aborts the process).

## The frame loop

- Drive the simulation with a Reanimated `useFrameCallback`; step the game model
  with the frame's delta time, then push results into Skia-readable shared values.
- No `setState`, no allocation-heavy work, no object churn inside the frame loop.
  React re-renders happen only on discrete events (death, capture score tick).

## Core geometry (the heart of the game)

Capture is decided by **closest approach** of the ball's straight flight line to a
planet center (point-to-line distance):

- closest approach < planet radius → crash (death)
- planet radius ≤ closest approach ≤ ring radius → capture into orbit
- beyond ring radius → sails past; off-screen with no capture → lost (death)

To keep this fair, a short dashed tangent aim line is drawn from the ball while
orbiting (rendering-only — the sim never reads it). Crash deaths were briefly
removed, then restored together with the aim line: the line fixes the
confusion-difficulty, crashes keep the aiming skill.

Scoring is split from progression: `planetsPassed` drives every difficulty
dial and zones; `score` is points. Never feed `score` into difficulty. Planet
ids are chain ordinals: `planetsPassed` = the highest id reached (skips
advance it fully; captures at/below the high-water mark score zero — prevents
bounce-farming).

Points per capture = `(capture + quick/perfect/graze flat bonuses) × (1 + heat)`.
`heat` (0–4) is the one multiplier and it is momentum: +1 per planet flown past
mid-jump (flyby = ball clears the top of a higher planet's ring — it can never
fire for the planet being landed on), −1 per half revolution camped, and −1 on
any landing that skipped nothing (applied after the payout). Holding red-hot
means skipping on nearly every hop. Heat also drives the comet visuals (ball
glow / trail color via `HEAT_COLORS`), the HUD ×N badge, and the SFX pitch —
one variable, four consumers.

On capture: orbit direction follows approach direction; the orbit starts exactly
at the capture point and settles out to the planet's fixed ring over
`CAPTURE_SETTLE_S`, blending angular speed from the (capped) flight speed down to
the orbit speed — position and velocity stay continuous, no snap. Release
velocity is the orbit tangent.

Camera: climbing is snappy; while orbiting it may also ease back down to the
anchor (sideways hops can put the anchor below the camera). It never moves down
during flight — downward flights fall out of the viewport and die.

## Tuning

All feel/difficulty numbers (speeds, radii, band widths, decay rates, spacing)
live in `src/game/constants.ts` as named constants — never inline magic numbers.
Difficulty dials are pure functions of `planetsPassed`.
